  "slug": "dark-energy-543b",
  "summary": "New reconstructions, model comparisons, independent SN Ia pipelines and CMB forecasts test the DESI-driven w0–wa departure from ΛCDM from several directions.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is whether DESI BAO or full-shape data, combined with CMB, supernova and lensing probes, require dark energy that departs from a cosmological constant. At the model level, three one-parameter vacuum histories are compared within VCDM: F42 is favored by AIC when supernovae are included, while the Bayesian evidence is assessed separately. A DESI DR1 full-shape analysis finds that the scalar-field Fading Dark Sector model outperforms CPL w0–wa. Two non-parametric reconstructions of the effective dark-energy density and gravitational response, using CMB, BAO, SNe and LSS or DES lensing data, report a phantom-divide crossing near z≈0.5–0.7, and one also asks whether dark energy clusters. On systematics, LEMAITRE and a ZTF/SNLS/HSC calibration effort provide SN Ia datasets independent of existing compilations to test the w–w_a tension. A Bayesian forecast examines whether Simons Observatory and CMB-S4 can distinguish w0waCDM from ΛCDM shadow models. Separate preprints address 21 cm signatures of boosted dark matter and the influence of astrophysical priors on new-physics evidence in pulsar timing arrays.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-23"
}
